          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Generally good, and it is the most useful setting on the machine for this purpose: it raises cardiovascular work substantially without raising impact at all. The caveat is that steep grades shift load toward the quadriceps and the front of the knee, so build from 2 to 3% a percent at a time rather than starting steep.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should I use the decline function if I have knee pain?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. Downhill walking loads the knee eccentrically — the muscles lengthen under tension to control the descent — and it is the most reliable way to aggravate an irritable knee. It is a genuinely useful function for preparing for a downhill race and the wrong function for this buyer entirely.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What speed should I walk at with bad knees?",
