        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Is a treadmill bad for bad knees?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not inherently — a cushioned deck typically loads the knee less than pavement does. What matters is speed, incline and how gradually you build. Walking generates roughly one and a half times body weight per footfall against two and a half to three times for running, so walking with incline is usually far better tolerated than running on any machine.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a softer treadmill deck better for your knees?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Up to a point, then it reverses. Very soft decks absorb the elastic energy your lower leg returns on each stride, so every step costs more muscular work and you fatigue faster — and a tired, deteriorating gait is what loads a bad knee badly. Look for measurable give with a quick return to shape, not maximum softness.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is incline walking good or bad for knees?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Generally good, and it is the most useful setting on the machine for this purpose: it raises cardiovascular work substantially without raising impact at all. The caveat is that steep grades shift load toward the quadriceps and the front of the knee, so build from 2 to 3% a percent at a time rather than starting steep.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Should I use the decline function if I have knee pain?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. Downhill walking loads the knee eccentrically — the muscles lengthen under tension to control the descent — and it is the most reliable way to aggravate an irritable knee. It is a genuinely useful function for preparing for a downhill race and the wrong function for this buyer entirely.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What speed should I walk at with bad knees?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Whatever feels smooth and controlled, which is often between 2 and 3 mph. This is why speed increments matter: machines that adjust in 0.5 mph steps cannot express the difference between a comfortable pace and one that lengthens your stride into discomfort. Look for 0.1 mph steps and a low minimum speed.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Would an elliptical or bike be better than a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Quite possibly, and it is worth considering honestly. A bike removes impact entirely; an elliptical is weight-bearing with no impact phase. If twenty minutes of flat walking is painful, a moving belt does not change the underlying loading. Weight-bearing exercise has bone-density benefits where it can be tolerated, which is the argument on the other side.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

